WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.106
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit ten: Semiotics. Today you will distinguish defining from non-defining information about signs and meanings.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.190
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.447
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.145
Sign.

7
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.904
Sign means anything that stands for something else.

8
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:31.690
A red light is a sign meaning 'stop'.

9
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:34.599
Your turn. Say: Sign.

10
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:38.492
Sign.

11
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.309
Signifier.

12
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:45.987
Signifier means the physical form of a sign: a sound, image or word.

13
00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:48.560
The word 'tree' is a signifier.

14
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.524
Your turn. Say: Signifier.

15
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:55.554
Signifier.

16
00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:57.376
Signified.

17
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:01.625
Signified means the concept a signifier refers to.

18
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:04.959
The signified is the idea of a tree in our mind.

19
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.144
Your turn. Say: Signified.

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.117
Signified.

21
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:13.710
Symbol.

22
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:17.796
Symbol means a sign whose meaning rests on convention.

23
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:20.313
The dove is a symbol of peace.

24
00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:23.465
Your turn. Say: Symbol.

25
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:26.930
Symbol.

26
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:28.510
Code.

27
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:32.747
Code means a shared system of rules for interpreting signs.

28
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.280
Dress codes communicate status.

29
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:38.106
Your turn. Say: Code.

30
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:41.736
Code.

31
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:43.542
Denotation.

32
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.211
Denotation means the literal meaning of a sign.

33
00:01:47.817 --> 00:01:49.956
The denotation of 'rose' is a flower.

34
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.435
Your turn. Say: Denotation.

35
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.382
Denotation.

36
00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.327
Connotation.

37
00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:04.209
Connotation means the associated or emotional meaning of a sign.

38
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:07.153
'Rose' carries connotations of love.

39
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.344
Your turn. Say: Connotation.

40
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.294
Connotation.

41
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.109
Interpretant.

42
00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:20.893
Interpretant means the understanding a sign produces in its receiver.

43
00:02:21.483 --> 00:02:24.098
The interpretant varies from one culture to another.

44
00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:27.243
Your turn. Say: Interpretant.

45
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.252
Interpretant.

46
00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:36.228
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:41.233
Visal, you chose the lotus for your semiotics project. Why?

48
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:51.664
Because the lotus, which grows out of muddy water, has strong connotations of purity in Cambodian culture.

49
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.352
So its denotation and connotation are quite different.

50
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:08.821
Exactly. The denotation is simply a water plant; the signs that really interest me are the ones whose meanings depend on culture.

51
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:12.590
Would a European reader understand it the same way?

52
00:03:13.683 --> 00:03:22.338
Probably not. Without the code that our culture shares, the same signifier can produce a completely different interpretant.

53
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.931
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:29.606
Grammar focus: Defining vs non-defining clauses.

55
00:03:29.906 --> 00:03:31.127
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.817 --> 00:03:35.337
A sign, which links form to meaning, is central to semiotics.

57
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.178
The signs that interest me are the ones shaped by culture.

58
00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:43.360
The lotus, which grows out of mud, symbolises purity.

59
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:59.621
Defining clauses (no commas) tell us WHICH one; remove them and the meaning changes. Non-defining clauses (with commas) add extra facts; remove them and the sentence still works. Compare: 'Signs that are cultural vary' vs 'Signs, which are cultural, vary'.

60
00:04:00.383 --> 00:04:05.404
Be careful! Don't say: A sign which links form to meaning is central.

61
00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:10.400
Say: A sign, which links form to meaning, is central.

62
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.827
Extra information needs commas.

63
00:04:13.633 --> 00:04:16.193
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.605
Reading the World as Text.

65
00:04:18.905 --> 00:04:24.747
Semiotics treats almost everything, from traffic lights to clothing, as a sign that can be read.

66
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:32.447
Saussure divided the sign into the signifier, which is its form, and the signified, which is the concept it evokes.

67
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:38.637
The connection between the two is usually arbitrary, so meaning depends on shared cultural codes.

68
00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:45.278
Signs that seem natural, such as colours for mourning, in fact differ widely between societies.

69
00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:51.227
Learning to read connotation, therefore, is a key skill in an image-saturated age.

70
00:04:52.333 --> 00:04:56.277
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.117 --> 00:05:00.022
Choose the sentence where the clause adds EXTRA information.

72
00:05:00.322 --> 00:05:08.155
A, The signs that we share are cultural.. B, Colours that mean danger are bright.. C, The lotus, which grows in mud, means purity..

73
00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.604
The answer is C: The lotus, which grows in mud, means purity..

74
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.224
The emotional associations of a word are its blank.

75
00:05:20.524 --> 00:05:24.456
A, denotation. B, connotation. C, signifier.

76
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.383
The answer is B: connotation.

77
00:05:31.517 --> 00:05:35.978
The physical form of a sign, such as a sound or image, is the blank.

78
00:05:36.278 --> 00:05:40.477
A, signified. B, interpretant. C, signifier.

79
00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.277
The answer is C: signifier.

80
00:05:47.417 --> 00:05:49.230
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.285
A, carry connotations. B, hold up connotations. C, do connotations.

82
00:05:58.833 --> 00:06:01.483
The answer is A: carry connotations.

83
00:06:02.617 --> 00:06:07.147
According to the reading, the link between signifier and signified is usually…

84
00:06:07.447 --> 00:06:11.565
A, arbitrary. B, natural. C, universal.

85
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.680
The answer is A: arbitrary.

86
00:06:18.867 --> 00:06:22.401
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.631
What is a symbol in your culture that carries strong connotation?

88
00:06:43.417 --> 00:06:44.963
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.263 --> 00:07:17.146
In Cambodian culture, the image of Angkor Wat carries an especially strong connotation. Its denotation is simply a temple, but it signifies national pride, history and resilience after years of conflict. That is why it appears on our flag, on banknotes and even on beer labels. Some people feel that such commercial use weakens its meaning. In my view, though, its constant presence shows how deeply the symbol is woven into our identity.

90
00:07:17.446 --> 00:07:19.295
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:20.183 --> 00:07:25.541
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and defining vs non-defining clauses.

92
00:07:25.841 --> 00:07:31.105
Today's value is cultural awareness. Notice the meanings your culture gives to signs and symbols.

93
00:07:31.405 --> 00:07:33.976
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:34.276 --> 00:07:37.602
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

